00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:23.029
going to be based on what the previous lesson had done

105
00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:24.731
for setting up a proposed schema

106
00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:26.466
for what kinds of facts and kinds of

107
00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.234
entities can be pulled out of the text.

108
00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:30.737
And then of course, to put all that stuff together,

109
00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:33.239
we're going to have a custom prompt that we pass in

110
00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:36.009
that's going to let the LLM know
exactly what we're looking for

111
00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:37.910
and how to think about doing the extraction.

112
00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:41.614
Let's walk through the end-to-end
workflow of the Neo4j

113
00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:45.318
KG Builder to help
you understand what these components do.

114
00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.854
First, each document is loaded.

115
00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:52.091
There's built in support for PDFs,
but you'll use a custom markdown loader.

116
00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.461
Next, a text splitter component will perform chunking.

117
00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:58.531
This is the common chunking
that happens in many different frameworks.

118
00:04:58.731 --> 00:04:59.832
For each chunk,

119
00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.368
the chunk embedder
will calculate a vector embedding.

120
00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.238
The chunk is then analyzed using an LLM,

121
00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:08.508
as instructed by the entity and relationship extractor.

122
00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:10.343
Now this component will be configured to

123
00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:12.445
align with our knowledge extraction plan.

124
00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:14.647
Once the graph has been constructed,

125
00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:17.650
an optional graph pruner
can be used to clean up the graph.

126
00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.585
This all happens in memory.

127
00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:23.556
A KG writer is the component responsible
for saving the in-memory graph

128
00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.224
into Neo4j itself.

129
00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:28.761
Finally, an entity resolver
component will merge nodes

130
00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:30.563
that are likely the same entity.

131
00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:35.068
Okay, so now you can start to define

132
00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:36.402
some of these custom functions we're going

133
00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.770
to need to pass into the pipeline.

134
00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:39.706
And the first thing we'll do

135
00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:41.140
is set up a custom text splitter.

136
00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:44.043
And if you remember the markdown files that we had,

137
00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.547
The markdown files had an H1 kind of header at the beginning,

138
00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.382
and then they had these page

139
00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.551
breaks basically for splitting up the reviews.

140
00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:54.520
And so we're going to use a
simple regular expression text splitter.

141
00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.224
So for Neo4J, there's this base class called TextSplitter,

142
00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:00.259
you can extend that with custom functionality. And

143
00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.328
so that's what we're going to do here.

144
00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:03.963
We're going to extend TextSplitter and

145
00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:05.898
then in the run function for TextSplitter

146
00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.500
because it's part of the pipeline.

147
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.203
Here's where our custom functionality is going to be put in place.

148
00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:12.205
We're just going to use a regular

149
00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:14.207
expression that's going to split the text

150
00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.709
based on this regular expression's been defined,

151
00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:19.011
that's going to be passed into the function itself.

152
00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:22.315
So for any kind of regular expression that's passed in,

153
00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.817
you can use that as the the split points within the text.

154
00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:27.487
The final part that it does here is

155
00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:29.389
go ahead and just use a list comprehension

156
00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:30.923
for taking each of those plain text

157
00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:32.392
splits and putting them into an object

158
00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:35.695
that the neo4j_graphrag library expects,

159
00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:37.797
called TextChunk.

160
00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.500
TextChunk will just have the text or the string itself,

161
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.702
an index for that particular chunk of text.

162
00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:44.837
And that's just going to turn into

163
00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.873
a single list with these TextChunk objects

164
00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:49.375
that the list is going to be called text chunks.

165
00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:53.880
You'll also be loading the markdown files
with a special data loader,

166
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.782
and similar to the text splitter,

167
00:06:55.782 --> 00:06:57.517
this is going to extend a

168
00:06:57.517 --> 00:06:59.585
base class from the Neo4J GraphRAG library.

169
00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:03.256
And there's a couple of these that are available,

170
00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:05.358
but we're going to define a special one here ourselves.

171
00:07:05.858 --> 00:07:08.461
So we're going to import the DataLoader base class

172
00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:10.096
and also these kind of types that

173
00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:11.964
actually need to be used for the output.

174
00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.299
We're going to make it look as

175
00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:14.667
if we have been parsing a PdfDocument,

176
00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:16.869
so we're going to load up a PdfDocument type.

177
00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:18.871
In this MarkdownDataLoader, the key thing that

178
00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:20.673
it's really going to be doing here

179
00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.876
is that we know that there's some
metadata within the markdown

180
00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:26.078
that we actually want to be able to extract

181
00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:27.947
and add to the context of the

182
00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:29.849
data loading when the chunking is happening.

183
00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:31.784
So we've got an extra utility

184
00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.553
function inside of the class
called extract_title.

185
00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:35.588
It's going to use a very basic

186
00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:37.557
regular expression to just kind of assume that

187
00:07:37.857 --> 00:07:40.560
the first time it finds a single H1 headline,

188
00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.062
it's going to use that as the title for the document.

189
00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:45.865
And so when the run function is called here,

190
00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:47.600
and the run is really the thing that's going to

191
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.702
load the data from some data source.

192
00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:51.604
So it's going to load from the file system.

193
00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:53.206
It's going to take the markdown

194
00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:54.807
text and just read the entire file,

195
00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:59.412
extract the title and then turn all
that into this DocumentInfo here.

196
00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:02.615
And it's the DocumentInfo
that really is the interesting part.

197
00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:06.118
This is the kind of the metadata for the document.

198
00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:08.621
That gets combined with the text of

199
00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:11.123
the document which we've loaded here
into markdown_text

200
00:08:11.424 --> 00:08:13.626
and put into this PdfDocument class.

201
00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.362
So the other things that you need to set up

202
00:08:16.362 --> 00:08:18.464
the knowledge graph construction pipeline

203
00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:21.367
is of course you need an LLM. We already we have that,

204
00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.769
but we're going to be using OpenAI again, of course.

205
00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.872
We're going to be using OpenAI for the embedder as well.

206
00:08:25.872 --> 00:08:27.874
And then we have to grab the Neo4J driver.

207
00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.943
So this is using the GraphRAG libraries for that.

208
00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:32.645
So Neo4J has support of course

209
00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:34.647
for OpenAI and other models as well.

210
00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:36.782
We're going to use OpenAI for both

211
00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.618
the LLM and also for the embeddings.

212
00:08:39.018 --> 00:08:40.920
So we're just going to create instances of those.

213
00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.322
LLM for Neo4J, the embedder as well.

214
00:08:43.623 --> 00:08:46.325
And we're going to grab the Neo4j driver

