00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:29.402
And for that, we're going to go ahead and extract from the

235
00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:31.203
approved_fact_types that we have.

236
00:09:31.203 --> 00:09:34.407
All those approved_fact_types
actually exist inside of a dictionary

237
00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:37.910
where the key in the dictionary is actually
the relationship type itself.

238
00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.413
And so we're just going to extract all the keys

239
00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:43.215
that should be the schema_relationship_types
and you can see them here.

240
00:09:43.215 --> 00:09:45.318
You're also going to use the approved_fact_types.

241
00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:48.187
but as what's called schema patterns

242
00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:49.922
by the knowledge graph builder pipeline.

243
00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:53.092
And it's really just a repackaging
of the exact same information.

244
00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:54.627
So from each of the facts, we're

245
00:09:54.627 --> 00:09:55.795
going to turn that into a list

246
00:09:55.795 --> 00:09:59.799
that has the subject label,
the predicate label, and the object label.

247
00:10:00.199 --> 00:10:01.667
And we're going to turn the predicate

248
00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:03.102
label, we're going to make that uppercase,

249
00:10:03.102 --> 00:10:05.171
which is the convention inside of Neo4J.

250
00:10:07.273 --> 00:10:10.910
The complete schema then required by
