00:19:50.789 --> 00:19:53.158
is what's the entity label that we're looking for.

497
00:19:53.659 --> 00:19:56.361
And now the match, rather than finding all nodes,

498
00:19:56.361 --> 00:19:59.464
we're only going to find nodes
that have that particular entity label.

499
00:19:59.665 --> 00:20:01.066
And here we're going to kind

500
00:20:01.066 --> 00:20:02.668
of turn things around a little bit.

501
00:20:03.068 --> 00:20:05.771
We actually want to look, of course, only

502
00:20:05.771 --> 00:20:08.473
where that label co-occurs with the __Entity__ label.

503
00:20:09.074 --> 00:20:11.109
And then, rather than finding the distinct

504
00:20:11.109 --> 00:20:13.178
set of labels, we're going to find

505
00:20:13.178 --> 00:20:16.982
distinct set of keys on those nodes and return those.

506
00:20:17.182 --> 00:20:18.584
And similar to what we did before,

507
00:20:18.584 --> 00:20:20.185
we're going to unwind those lists of keys

508
00:20:20.185 --> 00:20:22.387
into just a couple of rows of keys.

509
00:20:22.688 --> 00:20:24.690
So that's what the UNWIND is doing here.

510
00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:27.392
And then we're going to collect those all back into a list

511
00:20:27.693 --> 00:20:29.394
and then return that as the single result.

512
00:20:29.995 --> 00:20:32.197
So, let's define that function
and then give it a run,

513
00:20:32.698 --> 00:20:35.200
looking just for the unique keys related to Product

514
00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:37.002
within the subject graph.

515
00:20:37.803 --> 00:20:39.805
Okay, and it looks like

516
00:20:39.805 --> 00:20:42.507
we we didn't really constrain that too much

517
00:20:42.507 --> 00:20:44.610
when we were doing the entity extraction. So this time

518
00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:46.712
around, the LLM has actually found lots of different keys

519
00:20:46.712 --> 00:20:48.513
when it created those entities.

520
00:20:48.814 --> 00:20:50.515
So, across the different products,

521
00:20:50.515 --> 00:20:52.351
here's all the different kinds of properties

522
00:20:52.351 --> 00:20:54.219
that it actually derived from the reviews.

523
00:20:54.219 --> 00:20:56.521
It figured out things like the material,

524
00:20:56.521 --> 00:20:58.523
different features about, I don't know,

525
00:20:58.523 --> 00:21:00.525
the shelf depth apparently appears somewhere.

526
00:21:00.626 --> 00:21:01.827
You'd have to look at the

527
00:21:01.827 --> 00:21:03.228
text of the markdown to actually see

528
00:21:03.228 --> 00:21:05.530
why the LLM discovered these different properties.

529
00:21:05.731 --> 00:21:07.532
Depending on the particular review that

530
00:21:07.532 --> 00:21:09.635
was being used for during the extraction,

531
00:21:09.935 --> 00:21:11.570
some of the reviews probably have some

532
00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:13.438
of these properties being mentioned, others will not.

533
00:21:13.639 --> 00:21:16.341
And so this is not going to be
consistent across all properties,

534
00:21:16.842 --> 00:21:18.443
but some of these things probably are,

535
00:21:18.644 --> 00:21:21.246
particularly I'm thinking the name is going to co-occur.

536
00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.515
And you'll see later how we actually

537
00:21:23.515 --> 00:21:25.484
try to take this list of keys

538
00:21:25.484 --> 00:21:28.287
and correlate them with keys
that are available on the domain graph,

539
00:21:28.287 --> 00:21:30.389
and that's how we're actually going to sync everything up.

540
00:21:31.089 --> 00:21:33.458
The next utility function that you will define

541
00:21:33.458 --> 00:21:34.860
is very similar, but now we're going

542
00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.461
to turn our attention to the domain graph.

543
00:21:37.062 --> 00:21:39.264
And on the domain graph, we already know

544
00:21:39.264 --> 00:21:42.367
how to find the labels that are part of the domain graph.

545
00:21:42.367 --> 00:21:45.170
We know that they're labels where there's not

546
00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:47.472
an __Entity__ extra label on it.

547
00:21:47.839 --> 00:21:50.742
So if we match for a particular label,
let's say for Product,

548
00:21:50.742 --> 00:21:53.445
if we're going to find the unique domain keys for Product,

549
00:21:53.445 --> 00:21:55.347
we're going to head and match for that,

550
00:21:55.347 --> 00:21:58.450
and then filter out any of the ones
that have __Entity__

551
00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:00.252
because those are part of the subject graph.

552
00:22:00.752 --> 00:22:03.055
And then same as we did for the previous function that

553
00:22:03.055 --> 00:22:05.357
we're then going to go ahead
and get the unique keys

554
00:22:05.557 --> 00:22:08.760
on those domain nodes and then collect those up into a list.

555
00:22:09.061 --> 00:22:10.696
And so if we look for those

556
00:22:10.696 --> 00:22:12.364
unique property keys on the domain labels,

557
00:22:12.364 --> 00:22:14.499
this should be consistent because these were all

558
00:22:14.499 --> 00:22:16.635
of course imported from a single CSV file.

559
00:22:16.935 --> 00:22:19.004
It's a much smaller list. It probably

560
00:22:19.004 --> 00:22:21.406
correlates exactly with what the CSV file had.

561
00:22:21.506 --> 00:22:24.509
It's the product name, the price,
the description, product ID.

562
00:22:27.012 --> 00:22:28.880
To help that process along, we're going

563
00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:30.716
to define a helper function called normalize_key.

564
00:22:30.916 --> 00:22:33.418
You can just pass in for a particular label

565
00:22:33.418 --> 00:22:35.721
what is the key that you want to normalize here.

566
00:22:36.221 --> 00:22:37.923
And what this function's going to do, it's

567
00:22:37.923 --> 00:22:39.624
going to go ahead and lowercase the key,

568
00:22:39.624 --> 00:22:42.527
it's going to remove extra white space
that's not very helpful.

569
00:22:43.028 --> 00:22:45.530
And if the prefix is actually present there

570
00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:48.734
where the prefix is the label itself,
go ahead and remove that.

571
00:22:49.334 --> 00:22:50.702
would do things like this product

572
00:22:50.702 --> 00:22:52.337
name would end up being just name.

573
00:22:52.537 --> 00:22:55.340
Then you could obviously make it very easy
to correlate with the

574
00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:57.743
name key from the subject graph.

575
00:22:58.143 --> 00:23:01.747
So, Product_name would end up being just name,

576
00:23:01.747 --> 00:23:03.949
'product name' would be just name.

577
00:23:03.949 --> 00:23:05.384
And of course, a property key

578
00:23:05.384 --> 00:23:07.052
like price would still just be price.

579
00:23:07.052 --> 00:23:09.254
That wouldn't get changed at all.

580
00:23:09.254 --> 00:23:11.857
So again, the implementation is pretty straightforward,

581
00:23:11.857 --> 00:23:13.925
but the purpose of this is actually just to

582
00:23:13.925 --> 00:23:16.228
kind of make the property keys themselves easy to compare.

583
00:23:16.661 --> 00:23:17.896
And then you can look at some

584
00:23:17.896 --> 00:23:19.364
of the examples just for a sanity checking.

585
00:23:19.598 --> 00:23:22.100
That looks good.

586
00:23:22.401 --> 00:23:25.003
The next utility function that you'll put together

587
00:23:25.103 --> 00:23:27.406
is for correlating keys for a given label.

588
00:23:27.606 --> 00:23:29.307
Now, this is going to take advantage of

589
00:23:29.307 --> 00:23:31.009
some of the utility functions we already had.

590
00:23:31.176 --> 00:23:33.478
And let's take a look at the implementation.

591
00:23:33.979 --> 00:23:37.082
We're going to import a new library here
in Python called rapidfuzz.

592
00:23:37.582 --> 00:23:40.285
It's really just used for doing text similarity scoring

593
00:23:40.285 --> 00:23:42.788
and this is a simple edit-based scoring.

594
00:23:43.188 --> 00:23:45.223
There's a couple different ways for actually trying to figure

595
00:23:45.223 --> 00:23:47.292
out whether text strings are very similar to each other.

596
00:23:47.492 --> 00:23:49.795
rapidfuzz is a pretty good library for that,

597
00:23:50.028 --> 00:23:53.131
inside of the function itself,
given a particular label,

598
00:23:53.131 --> 00:23:54.666
some keys from the entity node

599
00:23:54.666 --> 00:23:56.435
and some keys from the domain node,

600
00:23:56.435 --> 00:23:58.069
we're then going to look at

601
00:23:58.069 --> 00:23:59.938
a similarity score that is a threshold

602
00:23:59.938 --> 00:24:03.041
for like how do these keys correlate
with each other according to

603
00:24:03.041 --> 00:24:06.845
the scoring from rapidfuzz's fuzz function.

604
00:24:06.845 --> 00:24:09.448
And then for the keys that correlate very highly,

605
00:24:09.448 --> 00:24:12.250
we're going to pair those up as saying
these keys are probably

606
00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:14.052
worth comparing the values of.

607
00:24:14.586 --> 00:24:16.588
Okay, let's look at this in detail.

608
00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:19.324
The correlated_keys,
so the keys that we want

609
00:24:19.324 --> 00:24:21.092
to pair up, start off as being empty.

610
00:24:21.393 --> 00:24:23.195
And we're going to iterate through all

611
00:24:23.195 --> 00:24:24.963
of the keys in the entity_keys collection.

612
00:24:25.664 --> 00:24:26.998
In kind of classic style, we're going to

613
00:24:26.998 --> 00:24:28.467
have a for loop inside of a for loop.

614
00:24:28.567 --> 00:24:30.669
We'll loop through all the entity_keys,

615
00:24:30.669 --> 00:24:33.271
and then inside of that,
we'll loop through all the domain_keys.

616
00:24:33.371 --> 00:24:35.040
And for each pairing there, we're

617
00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:36.975
going to consider how closely they correlate.

618
00:24:37.409 --> 00:24:40.045
We're going to go ahead and just normalize
each of those keys.

619
00:24:40.378 --> 00:24:42.414
And because the rapidfuzz similarity score

620
00:24:42.414 --> 00:24:44.115
goes from zero up to 100,

621
00:24:44.416 --> 00:24:46.318
we're actually going to flip that around so

622
00:24:46.318 --> 00:24:48.186
that it's looks more like a similarity score.

623
00:24:48.787 --> 00:24:51.823
And we're going to go ahead
and calculate the ratio of this

624
00:24:52.324 --> 00:24:55.026
normalized domain key and the entity key,

625
00:24:56.228 --> 00:24:57.829
that should be the fuzzy_similarity.

626
00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.432
Now, we want that fuzzy_similarity to be greater than

627
00:25:00.432 --> 00:25:03.134
the similarity threshold
that was passed into the function up here,

628
00:25:04.536 --> 00:25:07.439
which is currently set at 0.9 as a default.

629
00:25:07.839 --> 00:25:09.641
And so if it surpasses that threshold,

630
00:25:09.941 --> 00:25:12.143
we're going to go ahead
and add that to the correlated_keys

631
00:25:12.511 --> 00:25:14.412
as a pair that we think is valid.

632
00:25:15.313 --> 00:25:17.516
We'll go ahead and just sort those keys then

633
00:25:18.016 --> 00:25:20.619
just so it's easier to take a look at

634
00:25:20.619 --> 00:25:22.921
the ones that are the most highly correlated
versus less correlated.

635
00:25:23.021 --> 00:25:24.756
And we're going to take the top

636
00:25:24.756 --> 00:25:26.525
correlated one later on to actually use.

637
00:25:26.925 --> 00:25:29.728
So after defining that function,
we're going to go ahead

638
00:25:29.728 --> 00:25:33.131
and this part here is just to be trying it out.

639
00:25:35.133 --> 00:25:37.335
So let's define the function for product.

640
00:25:37.335 --> 00:25:39.738
It's going to go ahead and
find all the unique entity keys,

641
00:25:39.738 --> 00:25:42.007
find the domain keys, pass it into this function,

642
00:25:42.007 --> 00:25:43.808
and see the results.

643
00:25:44.609 --> 00:25:46.344
So for product, we can see

644
00:25:46.344 --> 00:25:48.313
that name and product name correlate perfectly.

645
00:25:48.613 --> 00:25:50.315
Price and price correlate perfectly.

646
00:25:50.315 --> 00:25:52.317
description and description correlate perfectly.

647
00:25:52.951 --> 00:25:56.054
But design to description,
that's pretty low correlation,

648
00:25:56.054 --> 00:25:58.223
dimensions and description, low correlation.

649
00:25:58.223 --> 00:26:00.125
Those aren't super great.

650
00:26:00.425 --> 00:26:02.227
What was the threshold that we passed in?

651
00:26:02.427 --> 00:26:04.729
Yeah, we we asked for similarity of 0.5.

652
00:26:04.729 --> 00:26:06.131
You can try different values here to

653
00:26:06.131 --> 00:26:07.732
see what kind of different results you get.

654
00:26:08.233 --> 00:26:10.702
The default value of 0.9 is one of those

655
00:26:10.702 --> 00:26:13.138
classic kind of high thresholds you want to set.

656
00:26:13.505 --> 00:26:16.308
Okay. One more bit of background here

657
00:26:16.308 --> 00:26:18.577
and then we're going to actually put all this stuff together.

658
00:26:18.944 --> 00:26:21.479
We've now been able to correlate for a given label

659
00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:23.448
in the subject graph and in the

660
00:26:23.448 --> 00:26:25.383
domain graph in the keys on those labels,

661
00:26:25.684 --> 00:26:28.353
what the good pairings are for actually trying to decide

662
00:26:28.353 --> 00:26:30.555
whether two nodes happen to be the same node.

663
00:26:31.022 --> 00:26:32.958
Now, this is all by way of having

664
00:26:32.958 --> 00:26:34.859
what's called entity resolution. This is a

665
00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:37.662
technique for achieving that.
There's many different techniques.

666
00:26:37.662 --> 00:26:40.365
This is a pretty good baseline and it works perfectly

667
00:26:40.365 --> 00:26:42.267
for this current data set that we have.

668
00:26:42.667 --> 00:26:44.069
Probably for a different data set, you're

669
00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:45.470
going to want to have multiple techniques.

670
00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:47.372
And of course, you could imagine an entire

671
00:26:47.372 --> 00:26:49.074
agent built around just trying to figure out

672
00:26:49.074 --> 00:26:51.042
for the data that you have,
what's the right technique

673
00:26:51.042 --> 00:26:53.044
for actually doing the resolution.

674
00:26:53.945 --> 00:26:56.247
But for what we're doing today,
we're going to make some assumptions.

675
00:26:56.648 --> 00:26:57.983
The next function that we actually

676
00:26:57.983 --> 00:26:59.517
want to use is actually when we're

677
00:26:59.517 --> 00:27:01.219
going through processing all the data

678
00:27:01.219 --> 00:27:03.221
inside of Neo4J with some Cypher calls.

679
00:27:03.555 --> 00:27:07.158
Cypher has some support for doing
string comparison methods as well.

680
00:27:07.659 --> 00:27:09.394
One of the value similarity functions

681
00:27:09.394 --> 00:27:11.463
that's available is called
the Jaro-Winkler distance,

682
00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.132
and that is really just a string comparison method,

683
00:27:14.432 --> 00:27:16.534
similar to you might use vector similarity.

684
00:27:16.534 --> 00:27:19.337
where you actually have to calculate the vector embeddings.

685
00:27:19.638 --> 00:27:22.240
Instead, you can figure out text distance
in lots of different ways.

686
00:27:22.741 --> 00:27:24.676
Jaro-Winkler does that by calculating what's

687
00:27:24.676 --> 00:27:26.945
called the edit distance between two strings.

688
00:27:27.545 --> 00:27:28.747
And it really looks at how

689
00:27:28.747 --> 00:27:30.115
many small edits have to be made

690
00:27:30.315 --> 00:27:32.517
to take some string A and some string B,

691
00:27:32.917 --> 00:27:34.486
how many edits do you have to make

692
00:27:34.486 --> 00:27:36.021
to actually make them be the same string.

693
00:27:36.621 --> 00:27:39.758
So the values end up being between zero and one

694
00:27:40.058 --> 00:27:41.926
where kind of in an inverse way, zero

695
00:27:41.926 --> 00:27:43.762
ends up meaning that it's an exact match

696
00:27:43.862 --> 00:27:47.265
and one means that there's no match
whatsoever between these two strings.

697
00:27:47.499 --> 00:27:51.336
Now, Neo4J's library of text similarity scores include

698
00:27:51.336 --> 00:27:54.105
this Jaro-Winkler calculation.
You can also use Hamming distance,

699
00:27:54.105 --> 00:27:56.408
the Levenshtein distance,

700
00:27:56.408 --> 00:27:59.511
there are Sørensen-Dice similarity and also fuzzy matching.

701
00:27:59.511 --> 00:28:00.979
And of course, if you want

702
00:28:00.979 --> 00:28:02.681
to, you can also do cosine similarity.

703
00:28:02.714 --> 00:28:04.883
using vector embeddings. All those have different,

704
00:28:04.883 --> 00:28:07.519
you know, features and
like everything else that we've been doing

705
00:28:07.519 --> 00:28:10.221
so far with this part of
the knowledge graph construction,

706
00:28:10.522 --> 00:28:12.123
you could probably pick and choose which one

707
00:28:12.123 --> 00:28:13.725
is better for given a particular dataset.

708
00:28:14.025 --> 00:28:15.927
For the dataset that we have, this Jaro-Winkler distance

709
00:28:15.927 --> 00:28:17.529
is going to be just fine.

710
00:28:17.896 --> 00:28:19.798
So you can take a look at what Jaro-Winkler

711
00:28:19.798 --> 00:28:21.700
would look like in action using just pure Cypher.

712
00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:23.902
If you look at the Cypher query

713
00:28:23.902 --> 00:28:25.704
that's happening here in the MATCH clause,

714
00:28:26.004 --> 00:28:28.073
we're actually going to be matching a pair

715
00:28:28.073 --> 00:28:30.408
of both entities and domains for a particular label.

716
00:28:30.809 --> 00:28:32.477
Here we're going to be looking for the

717
00:28:32.477 --> 00:28:34.112
entity label that is the same on both.

718
00:28:34.412 --> 00:28:36.114
And of course, the entity node

719
00:28:36.114 --> 00:28:38.116
also has the extra label of __Entity__.

720
00:28:38.683 --> 00:28:40.785
And then with the entity and the

721
00:28:40.785 --> 00:28:43.188
domain, and then calculating this Jaro-Winkler distance score,

722
00:28:43.688 --> 00:28:45.890
we're going to pass that on with a filter

723
00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:47.892
where the score has to be less than 0.4.

724
00:28:48.093 --> 00:28:50.895
If you remember,
the score of zero means it's a perfect match,

725
00:28:51.096 --> 00:28:52.297
and so we're going to have kind

726
00:28:52.297 --> 00:28:53.498
of the opposite way of scoring here.

727
00:28:53.998 --> 00:28:56.367
We're looking for low values. So filter on that

728
00:28:56.568 --> 00:28:58.570
and then return those values to take a look.

729
00:28:58.903 --> 00:29:00.505
So in this example, we're going

730
00:29:00.505 --> 00:29:02.407
to be passing in some query parameters.

731
00:29:02.707 --> 00:29:05.076
We're going to be looking for the entity label of Product,

732
00:29:05.376 --> 00:29:08.379
both on the subject graph and
also on the domain graph.

733
00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.281
On the subject graph, we're going

734
00:29:10.281 --> 00:29:11.883
to look for the entity key name,

735
00:29:12.183 --> 00:29:14.686
and the domain key is going to be product_name.

736
00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:17.422
Okay, you can see that these are perfect scores.

737
00:29:17.522 --> 00:29:19.524
Gothenburg table and Gothenburg table,

738
00:29:19.524 --> 00:29:21.526
these all look exactly the same, of course.

739
00:29:21.526 --> 00:29:23.528
So you would expect them to have a very low score.

740
00:29:23.595 --> 00:29:26.197
You can try this with different values to try to see

741
00:29:26.197 --> 00:29:27.365
what it would be like if

742
00:29:27.365 --> 00:29:28.700
you maybe had a little bit less

743
00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:31.803
strict threshold, like 0.5.

744
00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:36.074
you can see that the Gothenburg Table
and Västerås Bookshelf apparently

745
00:29:37.075 --> 00:29:40.779
are not terrible. Gothenburg Table
and Stockholm Chair.

746
00:29:40.879 --> 00:29:42.981
I don't know how those are actually very similar

747
00:29:42.981 --> 00:29:45.049
text-wise, but I guess the distance gives you that.

748
00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:47.652
This is why you usually want to end up

749
00:29:47.652 --> 00:29:50.054
with in practice having a very low threshold like 0.1

750
00:29:50.155 --> 00:29:51.589
so that they're as close as

751
00:29:51.589 --> 00:29:53.258
possible to being the actual same thing.

752
00:29:53.892 --> 00:29:56.094
So let's elaborate on that query a little bit.

753
00:29:56.094 --> 00:29:59.898
This is exactly the same query,
but we've added one extra part.

754
00:29:59.898 --> 00:30:03.401
Whenever we find two entities that have a very low threshold,

755
00:30:03.401 --> 00:30:05.703
so here we've repackaged things a little bit.

756
00:30:05.703 --> 00:30:07.639
Now in the WHERE clause, in

757
00:30:07.639 --> 00:30:09.908
this pairing of entities and domain nodes,

758
00:30:09.908 --> 00:30:11.943
we're going to have the WHERE clause actually

759
00:30:11.943 --> 00:30:14.012
just having a predicate on
the Jaro-Winkler distance.

760
00:30:14.312 --> 00:30:16.414
We're setting the threshold to be 0.1

761
00:30:16.915 --> 00:30:19.818
and for any pairs that pass that correspondence,

762
00:30:19.818 --> 00:30:22.821
we're actually going to go
ahead and create a relationship

763
00:30:22.821 --> 00:30:24.823
from the entity to the domain node. And

764
00:30:24.823 --> 00:30:27.125
so we're going to use the fixed type here,

765
00:30:27.125 --> 00:30:29.527
it's not going to be parameterized, of CORRESPONDS_TO.

766
00:30:29.527 --> 00:30:32.130
So the relationship will be this entity node

767
00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:34.299
corresponds with this domain node.

768
00:30:34.732 --> 00:30:37.135
Now, there's two sub-clauses that we're going to use here

769
00:30:37.135 --> 00:30:38.937
in case you decide to run this multiple times.

770
00:30:39.337 --> 00:30:41.739
This can be very useful. When you do a merge,

771
00:30:41.739 --> 00:30:44.542
merge inside of Neo4J is kind of like an upsert,

772
00:30:44.642 --> 00:30:46.511
and it actually has two sub-clauses

773
00:30:46.511 --> 00:30:48.213
based on the behavior of the upsert.

774
00:30:48.413 --> 00:30:51.216
So if the MERGE ends up finding a match

775
00:30:51.216 --> 00:30:52.917
for actually what you've described,

776
00:30:53.218 --> 00:30:55.220
then there's this sub clause for ON MATCH,

777
00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:58.323
and then whatever follows that will be executed.

778
00:30:58.623 --> 00:31:01.693
Or if you're doing the first time
and it's being created,

779
00:31:01.893 --> 00:31:03.995
the clause ON CREATE is being called.

780
00:31:04.696 --> 00:31:06.564
So after you do the MERGE, if the very

781
00:31:06.564 --> 00:31:08.600
first time you run it, ON CREATE will be called

782
00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:10.201
and we're going to set this

783
00:31:10.201 --> 00:31:11.703
value created at time with a timestamp.

784
00:31:12.036 --> 00:31:13.805
The other one, if you already have

785
00:31:13.805 --> 00:31:15.840
created it and you're just matching it again,

786
00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:18.243
we're going to say that updated_at also gets a timestamp

787
00:31:18.243 --> 00:31:20.511
and that'll be updated every time you run this again.

788
00:31:20.812 --> 00:31:24.015
Okay, and that runs across all of the corresponding products.

789
00:31:24.482 --> 00:31:26.017
That looks pretty good.

790
00:31:28.786 --> 00:31:30.955
That Cypher query is doing exactly what you want.

791
00:31:31.055 --> 00:31:33.258
So go ahead and wrap it inside of a function call,

792
00:31:33.358 --> 00:31:36.694
passing in the label, entity key, and the domain key.

793
00:31:37.295 --> 00:31:38.396
And also the similarity score that

794
00:31:38.396 --> 00:31:39.664
you want to have as a threshold.

795
00:31:40.164 --> 00:31:42.267
The query is pretty much the same here.

796
00:31:42.767 --> 00:31:44.369
and you can see that the parameters

797
00:31:44.369 --> 00:31:45.970
are being passed in as query parameters

798
00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:47.772
from the arguments to the function.

799
00:31:48.573 --> 00:31:50.475
And again, we can just go ahead and run that.

800
00:31:50.775 --> 00:31:53.278
We're trying that out on Product, name, and product_name.

801
00:31:53.778 --> 00:31:55.380
These are the two different keys

802
00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:58.082
from the entity and also from the domain graph.

803
00:31:58.283 --> 00:32:00.785
Great, so that found 10 relationships

804
00:32:00.785 --> 00:32:04.789
that made sense between the subject graph and the domain graph.

805
00:32:05.390 --> 00:32:09.294
So for completeness, while we know that we can connect the products,

806
00:32:09.294 --> 00:32:10.561
we want to do this for

807
00:32:10.561 --> 00:32:12.096
all of the entities that are available.

808
00:32:12.096 --> 00:32:14.032
So to correlate and connect all of

809
00:32:14.032 --> 00:32:16.267
the subject nodes to the corresponding domain nodes,

810
00:32:16.567 --> 00:32:18.670
we'll just put in a for loop that's going

811
00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:20.772
to go through all of the unique entity labels

812
00:32:21.072 --> 00:32:22.941
and then try to correlate them from

813
00:32:22.941 --> 00:32:25.076
the entity side over to the domain side.

814
00:32:25.276 --> 00:32:28.279
So the subject nodes and
the domain nodes will be connected.

815
00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:31.950
So you can see that Product was connected, of course.

816
00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:34.352
Location actually doesn't have any correlation.

817
00:32:34.752 --> 00:32:36.955
Neither does Issue or neither does Feature.

818
00:32:37.055 --> 00:32:38.756
But that's what we expected.

819
00:32:39.157 --> 00:32:40.558
What has happened now as a

820
00:32:40.558 --> 00:32:42.160
result of doing all of this work,

